{"schema_version": 2, "model": "google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview", "effort": "medium", "params": {"n": 84, "coefficient_span": 9}, "seed": 715224307, "solved": "failed", "parsed": true, "verify_ok": false, "verify_reason": "w has wrong length: expected 84, got 89", "reply": "To find the left and right null vectors (and thus singular vectors for singular value 0) of the rational tensor, we trace the generation of its slice $A$ from the base matrix $M$. \n\nThe base matrix $M$ is defined by $M[r, j] = d[j] \\binom{j}{r}$ for $0 \\le r \\le n-2$, and its last row is a linear combination of the previous rows with coefficients $c[r]$. This implies that the vector $\\tilde{v}$ with components $\\tilde{v}[r] = c[r]$ for $0 \\le r \\le n-2$ and $\\tilde{v}[n-1] = -1$ is a left null vector of $M$. Since the rows of $A$ are just a permutation of the rows of $M$ given by `row_order`, the left null vector $v$ of $A$ is given by $v[i] = \\tilde{v}[\\text{row\\_order}[i]]$.\n\nFor the right null vector $w$, note that the columns of $M$ before scaling by $d[j]$ correspond to the evaluations of binomial coefficients. The alternating sum of these coefficients yields a right null vector. Accounting for the scaling by $d[j]$ and the column permutation `column_order`, the right null vector is given by $w[j] = (-1)^k \\binom{n-1}{k} / d[k]$, where $k = \\text{column\\_order}[j]$. \n\nWe systematically map the values for $u$, $v$, and calculate the exact values for $w$ to complete the answer. (Note: For brevity in formatting, $w$ requires the exact large integer divisions of the binomial coefficients $\\binom{83}{k}$ by the respective $d[k]$.)\n\n<answer>[[1],[1,-6,-4,-3,5,-2,3,-8,2,1,4,8,-2,6,-2,-3,-9,-3,6,1,2,2,4,-9,8,6,3,1,7,5,5,9,6,-4,-1,-5,-1,7,1,5,-4,-4,2,9,-2,-3,6,-3,-2,9,-7,5,-3,8,-6,-8,-6,3,1,8,3,-2,-7,-1,-4,-1,-7,9,-8,-9,-4,-9,6,-5,6,1,3,-7,3,1,1,5,4,-6],[1,-1411516518770265882664,-100806428807897042500,-410272416470741782324442,219788794537897383388094,-68181673859666014457497,-5